cited_sources.text:
The Events API works for iOS, Android and cross-platform apps built with React Native, Expo or similar frameworks, where our script and NPM package can't run because there's no browser.

1. Add your app to your Plausible account as a new site. The domain doesn't need to be a real domain name, so you can use your app's name.
2. Send a `pageview` event each time a screen is shown, and custom events for the actions you want to count. Name your screens like page paths in the `url` (for example `app://localhost/settings`), and they appear in your **Top Pages** report like any other page.
3. Send the requests from the app itself. Plausible then uses the device's IP address, so you don't need to set `X-Forwarded-For`.

Set the `User-Agent` header to one that describes the device, such as the device's web view user agent. Without it, requests carry your HTTP library's default user agent, which leaves the **Devices**, **Browsers** and **Operating Systems** reports with little to show.

Here's an example in React Native:

```js
const PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN = 'myapp' // the name you added your app with in Plausible

export function trackScreen(screenName, props) {
  return fetch('https://plausible.io/api/event', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'User-Agent': deviceUserAgent // for example from getUserAgent() in react-native-device-info
    },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      name: 'pageview',
      url: `app://localhost/${screenName}`,
      domain: PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN,
      props
    })
  })
}
```

Call it whenever the visible screen changes, for example from your navigation library's state change listener. Since apps don't have a referrer, visits are counted under Direct / None unless you send a `referrer` or UTM tags in the `url`.

Community-built SDKs for some platforms are listed in our integration guides.

The Top Pages report lists your pages by their path, such as `/blog/2026/11/123`. When paths don't tell you what's on the page, or the same path shows different content over time, it helps to see the page title too. You can send the title of each page yourself with a custom property, then see your stats by title.

## 1. Add the page title to your tracking snippet

In your Plausible snippet, add a `customProperties` option to the `plausible.init` call:

```javascript
plausible.init({
  // ... Other initialization options
  customProperties: function() {
    return { title: document.title }
  }
})
```

This sends the title of the page, as it shows in the browser tab, with every pageview and every custom event. Using a function means the title is read at the moment each event is sent, so it stays right as visitors move between pages. On a single-page app, check a few titles in your dashboard after setting this up: if your app changes the title only after the address has changed, a pageview can carry the previous page's title.

If you use our NPM package, pass the same `customProperties` option to `init`.

## 2. Add the property in your site settings

Go to your site settings, open the **Custom Properties** section and add `title`. You can also add it with one click once the first pageviews with it have come in.

## 3. See your stats by page title

At the bottom of your dashboard, in the **Goals** section, open the **Properties** tab and choose `title`. You see your visitors and pageviews for each title.

Click a title to filter the whole dashboard by it, for example to see where the visitors to that page came from. With a title filter on, the Top Pages report shows which paths carry that title. More on filtering by custom properties.

A link counts as outbound when it leads to a different hostname. That includes your own subdomains: a click from `www.yoursite.com` to `shop.yoursite.com` is recorded as an outbound link click.

To leave those clicks out, add a `transformRequest` option to the `plausible.init` call in your snippet. Replace `yoursite.com` with your own domain:

```javascript
plausible.init({
  // ... Other initialization options
  transformRequest: function (payload) {
    if (payload.n === 'Outbound Link: Click' && payload.p && payload.p.url) {
      var host = new URL(payload.p.url).hostname
      if (host === 'yoursite.com' || host.endsWith('.yoursite.com')) {
        return null
      }
    }
    return payload
  }
})
```

Returning `null` drops the event before it's sent. Clicks to all other sites are recorded as before. Clicks that were already recorded stay in your stats: to leave them out of your reports, filter by the **url** property with "does not contain" and your domain.

